20.8 Reproducibility and assumptions
Rscript book/scripts/regenerate.R joint writes joint_core_sweep.csv (cell means and standard errors), joint_contrasts.csv (paired contrasts) and joint_optimiser.csv (the exchange-search trace) to book/data (no total run time was recorded; the exchange searches of the cells whose times are kept in Table 20.9 sum to 16.4 minutes, a lower bound on the run; a rerun was not compared byte for byte with the tracked files), and keeps the per-replicate results in report/joint_raw.rds (gitignored). Table 20.4, Table 20.5 and the misspecification check of Section 20.2 run at render time and assert their identities. tests/testthat/test-joint.R checks that:

every made cross gets exactly \(k\) plots and every unmade cross none;
at \(k = 1\), 12 x 12 pools and four locations, the coverage rule keeps location loads within one plot and gives a higher female coverage than random placement (female coverage only; neither male coverage nor \(k &gt; 1\), where Table 20.7 shows the cap is exceeded);
the cap excess is at most one plot at \(c = 2\), \(k = 4\) over seeds 1 to 40 (it does not assert zero);
joint_connectivity() agrees with a literal union-find count of n_comp_cross and n_comp_loc on 150 random 8 x 8 allocations at five locations;
joint_pev() equals the GCA block of the explicitly inverted coefficient matrix of sparse_blup();
the optimised allocation never scores worse than its start, whether the start is implicit or passed in;
n_comp_cross is two for NC II blocks at one location, in separate locations and in shared locations, and equals the bipartite count of design_components(); n_comp_loc is two only when the blocks are grown apart.

The numbers are findings of this configuration, not constants: they move with the budget, the pool sizes, the genetic correlation between locations and the SCA share.

22.2 Why a breeding programme needs a burn-in
The loop below advances every stage by one year, simultaneously. That only means something if all seven stages are already occupied, by cohorts of different ages: this year’s elite trial was crossed five years before this year’s crossing block (the cohort in the DH stage was crossed one year before it, the headrows two, the PYT three, the AYT four, the EYT five). Starting the loop from an empty programme would compare a year-1 elite trial that does not exist against a year-1 headrow that does.
The fix is the staggered fill in the course’s vignette, and it is the most confusing seven lines in it:
for (year in 1:7) {
  F1 &lt;- randCross(Parents, n_crosses)
  if (year &lt; 7) DH   &lt;- makeDH(F1, n_dh)
  if (year &lt; 6) HDRW &lt;- setPheno(DH, reps = rep_hdrw)
  if (year &lt; 5) PYT  &lt;- ...
  if (year &lt; 4) AYT  &lt;- ...
  if (year &lt; 3) EYT  &lt;- ...
}
Read it as a triangle rather than a loop. On the first pass every stage runs, so every stage holds a cohort. On the third pass the elite trial is skipped (it runs on the first two only), so the cohort sitting in it is older than the one behind it; the advanced trial is skipped from the fourth pass, the preliminary trial from the fifth, the headrows from the sixth and the DH production from the seventh. By the seventh pass only the crossing block still runs. What comes out is a programme in which each stage holds a cohort of the right age relative to its neighbours – a steady state, which is the only state in which a per-year genetic gain is a meaningful number. (The function below writes the thresholds as offsets of n_burn, which gives the same thresholds, 7 down to 3, at n_burn = 7.)
